2186535891 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a538906d-e42b-5de1-87a1-cf764b3258be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186535715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535908 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce1cd99e-e545-5f30-9775-38b6fad7af7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535926 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b0/b0e8cbe1a26b5a012f6098b67320070e882fa38783d8eda07dd0b76011a47b21.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ad0e9c9-a63e-55c4-a91b-b9b32de9ed5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535926 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5}{2}y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-\\frac{5}{2}y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-5y+2 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(2y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2},2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ to $y=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d5/d5067706f9ff1abbdfd9dfce94711cb854f79c1156ddc0d81c1c35a5ed555f7a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y.$\n\n![](images/a9/a944979bc352b0e40afea73836b22c1fb1513fabbc727fb60d4c96793ee6fbb5.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{5}{2}-y)-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1eb04cf2f3ae0669dd82106b064dd822da618e9ca31bb3b96e4e30c71b7c3193.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{1}{y})-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535928 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/64b0f84b62adc0db3385a75d8fdc6e6707eb6778084b9e21de863ca6954c4c1d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=25$ and $x+5y=25$ for $y\\in [0,5]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=25.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c99954c-8e68-5e26-befe-7d930f5b079f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535928 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+5y & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b7cafc0ad6ab7624c1274451f16ba2c3c9a111ac9dad3abd90f082cc42d9b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\n![](images/16/166dbb3f75000dfc155abfc3452ff6874dab1542409edc7e4867c5a5ee73c0b0.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[\\,(25)-(25-5y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[5y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/93/9322b6f4bc9a0e913670f8dcbc9657206c3ae6a30de2f26308054d75b67d0861.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(25)-(25-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}({(5y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}(25{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{25{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{25(5)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(5)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3125}{3}-625) \\\\  & =\\frac{1250\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535940 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535940 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535940 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535940 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ef/efe8084c2e5b9d5eff4f75dffc9acce77aa66ab0dc62c699589573d96f884d49.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-{x}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535940 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25934858-d636-5a89-8459-35c28b65815f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535940 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/45/4514019be21deb238c92f557cc041aec9b5e6629bc9d7dcac133a7aa2aed89c7.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={x}^{2}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/61/611d10f83c76467c69a1510809738611962dd5d1e1c22a10b0cb3081d2aa52cc.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(-{x}^{2})-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={x}^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/68/683244c0b5be598da85a8875708f459d4f87319677111f1072c997d911075d68.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,({x}^{2}-2)-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}((2-{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4-4{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4-4{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(4x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ((4(1)-\\frac{4(1)^{3}}{3})-(4(-1)-\\frac{4(-1)^{3}}{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi ((4-\\frac{4}{3})-(-4+\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi (8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535944 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ae/ae4ec1bfc4bf15f8ae4d79492389dd6a3fb5e3c504b0d99a43466fc08bd4b63e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ and $y={e}^{x+2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "417976f8-e97d-5f88-9ef3-7e599b484745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535944 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/74/742602117733f7e0f8fa89931ff11cdfa483c3d878a99445ec1408de6aea8f30.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={e}^{x+2}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={e}^{x+2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6d2a21d75b441ca5c7c76320aad11b4dded17052056f8828e5c93b068b9bad3.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({e}^{x+2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e84714cd64d30cbb4f73c93fc6dfdfeb12f10422f0524d754906709a9a26ff6.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({e}^{{x}^{2}})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-361829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535951 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/77/778041d84c40044d6f047203a017bd64c0569bcfc9442afca9baa5160b494ae7.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d03566e-e00c-5038-a04e-9a446a86a195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535951 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+1 & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =x \\\\ x & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=4.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7c/7ce02bca7667a9c32971b7840ff2ba4b96ef3a5c9b18aceaeb5b9b713cc7b36f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\n![](images/6c/6cb557a57a189a426b6cccacd599562332b05aace5bbbfc5d996b0d53fed3359.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\sqrt{x}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/26/26886afd61251cae273c086dba59dcfa912665cdb788b0c3ef866bf61907efa2.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\frac{x}{2}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(\\sqrt{x}+2)}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{2}+2)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x+4\\sqrt{x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{12}\\,{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,{x}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi ((-\\frac{1}{12}\\,(4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,(4)^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,(4)^{3/2})-(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (8) \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535957 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535957 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535957 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/90/90980bb04eb7dd9906ec9a99ea3a1b7cfd9c00969bbd9b12c60fe0f80eb88840.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+2{y}^{2}=1$ and $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3})$ for $y\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6be7f528-8a2e-5e94-a08f-bb7c33bc8c26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535957 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equation of our first curve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2{y}^{2} & =1\\,⟹\\,x=1-2{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/16/16ca1c96786eb8e7ef12608f70d03b34b679e63d34ef41135b4fd6801bcccc27.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}).$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}).$\n\n![](images/27/276dea7b2d9f50e3fe171cceb64c1c7a12f84cfb18bdbe242102802ad9790f49.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}))\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3})]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c6fb140bb3eaf4c49cba76af157fb46a568bc2dc24e4763facede62720f844.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(1-2{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[2{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}))}^{2}-{(2{y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535965 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535965 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535965 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535965 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ac/ac7f02919896aeb40859b1d78608ec7620c26ea77becc806a28d39ef8abacd12.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ for $y\\in [1,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535965 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88a251ee-6143-5e86-bf44-46f8b33042cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535965 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/76/76196a70cdbc65d2f91b4ba500ea83083be5cfe10fe3f3bd09c035e3ea7bcfba.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/e5/e5d8affefbde216fed0bf6acd7d464e921c1cf04e2578050d12c928d6dee5bd0.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3)-(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4002c7922eac8e7c97d402833eabc3621ed4da2d13799d07206a45cf0afd1f1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3)-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535969 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/43/43cff74165732892223841f6780e7aa22c024e3b3c173e60ce15d8d6841a5a08.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x$ for $x\\in [-2,\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "923c9707-06d1-5031-b8df-221476502c14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535969 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7a/7aad98fe0513514aa8827bea4e9ffbac5b70bcb8da7923effd789988c81e286f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}-1.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}-1.$\n\n![](images/62/623b59b7aca8b33c6149ac6b0916d1eeb425adc3f74568868383806cd9b03b12.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(5)-({x}^{2}-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/c0/c0467a3bf02287589f897586a1358e4abb02759134b6b58622125319dc7331da.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[\\,(5)-(-\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-348267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535970 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fc/fc70e0f7c7ac82e399e520faa39cf04d252501da46834e88c06d8eba2b8d192d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=16$ and $x+4y=16$ for $y\\in [0,4]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=16.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92412be4-49e1-54ee-8609-445cb6da4a1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535970 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =16\\,⟹\\,x=16-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+4y & =16\\,⟹\\,x=16-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f3/f39f9f399ea4d0e65a499f34d41158ddbaf5319a02e057e097b4f80c8197ba9e.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=16-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=16-4y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=16-4y.$\n\n![](images/47/47d985510b37b7a14e82dfc20bc136694e7ba2e17172b35be45ebde88d238677.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\,(16)-(16-4y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[4y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=16-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e5494005010f3cfb8fb8b116c4b8ca91e8d3bf2712ad45c60f0766a2021b19a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(16)-(16-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(4y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(16{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{16{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{16(4)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(4)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1024}{3}-\\frac{1024}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2048\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535976 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cf/cf2c500974f9c88751b9dec98cc53b5d3616b736f3ab0504adae5879cac2001a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}-2x$ and $y=3-3{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-\\frac{3}{5},1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2e19710-89b7-5129-86a4-6f0be5f110ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535976 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d5/d5593e8c1e1d544acb8824d9428823d3d6cef3971a1cedd4b087a7902b0e2239.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=3-3{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\n![](images/bd/bddc78f683e28ec6e97fddb32f95dfdaaff27d3aafbdfc61f424857916f81a6c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(5)-(2{x}^{2}-2x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=3-3{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc049c46593baf1d96c04b95680d6b444ab9ceffc64d1f23f310f70e78684c14.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(5)-(3-3{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535977 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/55/559548dc5f2570d1341760ab87a2619646ed6985fd6437cb29442dc58bc6fee4.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+2y-1$ and $x=-1-{y}^{2}$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2f8487e-855b-5e52-8eac-210ae0123755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535977 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y-1 & =-1-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=0.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8f/8f1bccafe64a6926bdb214410453cecafa7059f74a539b155a4a4e6cccd67d1a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2y-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=-1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=-1-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d1d1ed82fadf105997e00d39b99992cdeba1d41be13cf599db63b34902e149a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(-1-{y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2y-1.$\n\n![](images/04/040e0a87b4bb5c7121a27fd2cc94c520f76919afbb86a49cfed2ed0cf57c1561.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,({y}^{2}+2y-1)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y-1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}-{[(y+1)^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}-(y+1)^{4})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-350914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535983 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/80/8063ba9fbe4c569519eea27e5a5cd69a8f0e708b03d153942fbf22e9345af96a.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}+2$ and $y=20-{x}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=20.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b034c5b9-fc9c-52d3-a856-d07f183d8ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535983 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =20-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f1/f196ef4a3b29a9d62609b0a9290f34d2cb1ef1351baa4883441fd89e9345e4ed.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=20-{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}+2.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](images/c0/c006e66d82e0059b341520bebe983fa75ed144e3050ca8c4493a4b6df3e8f352.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(20)-({x}^{2}+2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[18-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=20-{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c91a8e5ed0881ed0c8f34fa2ed6ccb5f95ff75a2f3b48e2d340f8a8856885e3a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(20)-(20-{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[18-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}((18-{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}(324-36{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}(324-36{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(324x-12{x}^{3})|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\pi ((324(3)-12(3)^{3})-(324(-3)-12(-3)^{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi ((972-324)-(-972+324)) \\\\  & =\\pi (648-(-648)) \\\\  & =1296\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535984 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dc/dccfcb991a6c302c522ea73381544d2e6f93b7310f54040151a00832473596b8.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=8x$ and $y=-{x}^{4}$ for $x\\in [-2,0]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5e827a7-4590-5f6c-9029-38608edd38d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535984 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/33/335b300d66c7a51a37d595f5fb99a281c00ec6a5be62e1be12c2a3104cd54694.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-{x}^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=8x.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=8x.$\n\n![](images/aa/aae56ea01faa2c09f004fe2506c036f4d5e5b2e591e2e653f4794e4a3cecc309.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(1)-(8x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1-8x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-{x}^{4}.$\n\n![](images/63/635d3aabdc16fd90c2e2a06a042f98abe5dc16f1d7747394cd9083858023adee.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(1)-(-{x}^{4})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1-8x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({[1-8x]}^{2}-{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535988 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c2/c2a8da9dd6d088d30b0b023d50ebf2c23e2bcfda89cd390ec16779694ae5eb39.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=x$ in the first quadrant is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bfc9867e-24b3-5baa-adb5-18feba56d583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535988 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =x \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/82/82783afd970ec3f0e90a8b97a12207b0ed8cbf82323e0512e9fe36b99a6fdc2f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{3}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{3}.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a9a72a194226f73da234e03b93e4b255849315808ac948c0958d8dee52b4f73.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-({x}^{3})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=x.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c53b4b1ad169119cadf490636b12235b3fc225a08d57c11d4f8e26318797f25.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-(x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}-{[2-x]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535996 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535996 #edb/ref 1069 "q-361808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535996 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535996 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5a/5a242a5bec4be4cb58b77289ea2ccf49bf22a368c19ec18f10790a417bfbff72.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=3-x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=4.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535996 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d651054f-0a78-573d-a5b9-95d84b459c37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535996 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/25/255abcfb1a9034df297dc4939d945d8c3b1c4bd717cebde2844aa2a229fd5258.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=3-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b480a3bb7312a59edcabb133100bee1ebd5777664ea490b58744171e05e3083.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(4)-({x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=3-x.$\n\n![](images/20/20de93738463a5b04c0c5c0884d1db7d8867503931122c99a27fc82493b64edb.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(4)-(3-x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(4-{x}^{2})}^{2}-{(1+x)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}-2x+15)\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+15x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}(1)^{5}-3(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+15(1))-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}-3-1+15) \\\\  & =\\pi (11+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{55}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535998 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6ba207cd34725ec2e3995eb0f90e63bedc8573300335b2d31163457ef8ee9bad.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+1$ and $x=2{y}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db9dcd58-6ee1-5525-a1ac-d82114a9c0f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535998 #edb/ref 1074 "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =-1,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/41/41f8dff0676c5cf8952f46b174ff4f98deeb97eec272e417f145b4782770c156.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4d3d75dfab4aa6382bf47f06a57036ec612a05c90d0764cd48fdc1f150f2453.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,({y}^{2}+1)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/6f/6f79c95e2b2d3aaf71e6dfac5ca93fa20261f5f40e1660cefc4ac23af3e56cf8.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,(2{y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2{y}^{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[{y}^{2}+3]}^{2}-{[2{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3{y}^{4}-2{y}^{2}+5)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{3{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+5y)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+5\\cdot 1)-(-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{3}}{3}+5\\cdot (-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{112\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536001 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1c/1c22369a6f1abc89437de1977135208ef5d0e762a4971df1c9ace14b6af932cd.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{5y}{8}$ and $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd731e5d-ee05-5b06-811e-7a9b6ae9709a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536001 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{5y}{8} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =5y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-5y & =0 \\\\ y(2y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/50/504e204d73a1f87d72a321e7c0f58eadf01840ff3b8dbc5214f08e50fcbdcf9b.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{8}y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{8}y.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c43a01a8548112d8dc1f30f9059c560ac7bec1e679a6c76394d83e4bfbea668.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\,(\\frac{5}{8}y)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8478ef13983e6529fa10147f9659171e31a5555cb799ec1fad13d1fe8b25191.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\,(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}({[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536006 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b0/b0aa7d362596dcea976fead0467b9eb76c04b85c02c55dbd12e9a95652eda276.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ for $y\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc2bc671-1b50-5524-a44c-d196ad9a937f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536006 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x & \\,⟹\\,x=y \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f1/f15165acc18f4cf828671e3f9085274b26598a643be9eeb97be617272d7008d7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/54/54820b3b386c9a435de04e0b3f07d4e2221a3e026ba5ba93ca25fa6640aa0ab1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e1a6067500f81cabeac8dc43c4c585da608ea655667dc5e00f29a6fad0dda60.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[3-y]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(3-{y}^{2})}^{2}-{(3-y)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{4}-7{y}^{2}+6y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7{y}^{3}}{3}+3{y}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot {1}^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
